{
    "stages": {
        "prototype": {
            "level": 4,
            "rationale": "For isolated, throwaway exploration, speed and learning can outweigh close review, so full vibe coding is reasonable. Keep prototypes away from sensitive data and production systems, and discard or carefully rework the code before reuse."
        },
        "proof-of-concept": {
            "level": 3,
            "rationale": "A demo should behave reliably enough to support stakeholder decisions, so have a person check key paths and use tests while reviewing changes selectively. Treat the code as demonstrative rather than production-ready, especially around security, data handling, and claims about capability."
        },
        "mvp": {
            "level": 2,
            "rationale": "Real early users can encounter data loss, security problems, or costly failures, so review every change line by line and verify behavior before release. This creates a sound baseline while still letting AI draft implementation."
        },
        "production": {
            "level": 2,
            "rationale": "Paying customers and ongoing maintenance require code that fits the system's reliability, security, and operational needs. Review every change line by line, alongside tests and the team's normal release controls."
        },
        "critical": {
            "level": 1,
            "rationale": "For regulated or safety-critical systems, AI can help propose approaches, but qualified people should write or rewrite every line under the required engineering and compliance processes. Use domain-specific validation, traceability, and independent review; AI-generated code should not bypass them."
        }
    },
    "summary": "Allow full vibe coding only for isolated throwaway prototypes. As software moves toward real users and higher impact, increase human review and verification; critical systems should use AI as an adviser, with established assurance processes governing all code."
}
